        <div id="three-bluetooths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Three Different Things Called “Bluetooth”</h2>
<p>
            A listing that says "Bluetooth treadmill" could mean any of three features, and many
            buyers discover after delivery that they bought a different one from the one they wanted.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. Bluetooth audio (the speaker)</h3>
          <p>
            Your phone pairs with the treadmill as it would with a portable speaker, and the console
            plays its audio — music, a podcast, the sound of a class video on your phone. This is what
            the Goplus listings mean by "Bluetooth speaker", what Horizon means by "Bluetooth allows you
            to stream media right to the built-in speakers", and what Sole means by "dual Bluetooth
            speakers". It needs nothing but the phone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. Bluetooth data (the app connection)</h3>
          <p>
            The treadmill sends speed, distance and sometimes incline to an app, and on some machines
            the app can change the speed. Budget machines usually connect only to the maker's own app
            (Gymax for Goplus, <a href="/urevo-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>UREVO</a>'s app, SunnyFit). Better machines also talk to third-party apps;
            Horizon's T101 listing names JRNY, Peloton and Zwift. The standard that lets a treadmill
            work with many apps is called FTMS (Fitness Machine Service). A listing that names Zwift or
            Kinomap compatibility is usually using it; a listing that only names its own app may not
            be. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for Zwift</a> guide covers
            this in detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. Bluetooth heart-rate receiver</h3>
          <p>
            The console reads a chest strap or armband and shows your heart rate, and on some machines
            runs heart-rate programmes. The Horizon T101 and Sole F63 listings both mention a Bluetooth
            heart-rate receiver. The Goplus machines rely on <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>grip sensors</a> or the app.
          </p>
          <p>
            The point of separating these is practical. If you want to play music without earbuds, any
            of the five machines here will do it. If you want a treadmill that runs <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a> or Peloton
            from a tablet, only the Horizon on this page names those apps in its listing. If you want
            both, buy for the app connection first; a speaker is a far easier feature to add with a
            $30 portable unit than app compatibility is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speaker-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How Good Is a Treadmill Speaker?</h2>
<p>
            Honestly, modest. Treadmill speakers are small drivers set into a plastic console, chosen to
            fit the space and the budget. They are fine for a podcast at walking pace and for music at
            an easy jog. At running pace they compete with three sources of noise that sit much closer
            to them than to you: the motor under the hood, the belt slapping over the deck, and your
            own footfalls. On a budget pad with the speaker in the motor cover, the belt is inches away.
          </p>
          <p>
            That does not make the speaker pointless. It means it is best thought of as a convenience
            for walking, warm-ups and classes where you mostly need to hear an instructor, rather than
            a replacement for headphones on a hard run. Three things help:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Turn the phone up, not just the console.</strong> Most treadmill volume controls
            sit downstream of the phone's own volume, so a quiet phone gives a quiet speaker.</li>
            <li><strong>Reduce the noise you can control.</strong> A dense mat under the frame cuts deck
            noise transmitted through the floor, and a correctly tensioned, lubricated belt is quieter
            than a dry one. Our <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills</a> guide covers what
            actually makes a treadmill loud.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the phone close to the console.</strong> Bluetooth range is rarely a
            problem, but a phone in a pocket on a moving body can drop the connection briefly; the
            phone or tablet holder on every machine here is the better place for it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you share the room or the house, a speaker also has a cost: the sound goes everywhere.
            For early-morning runs in a flat, headphones are kinder to the neighbours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="3hp-listing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The “3HP Folding Electric Treadmill With Bluetooth Speaker” Listings</h2>
<p>
            The search phrase "3hp electric folding treadmill with bluetooth speaker", followed by a
            colour such as purple or red, mostly leads to one machine family: the Goplus 3HP Electric
            Folding Treadmill, sold under at least three ASINs that share the same title and description
            at $299.99. Its bullets describe a 3.0 HP motor, speeds from 0.6 to 8 mph with two preset
            buttons for 3 and 5 mph, an HD touch screen, the Gymax app, a retractable phone holder, a
            Bluetooth speaker, a knob-operated fold that stands up on its own without leaning on a wall,
            and transport wheels. It arrives without assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Similar phrases also return other brands that use "3HP" and "Bluetooth speaker" in long
            keyword titles — on the day we checked, BORGUSI, KALWOL, SereneLife and others. Some of those
            listings state more figures than Goplus does, but they come from sellers with a shorter
            track record, and we have not included them. If you are comparing one, look for the same
            essentials as for any treadmill: a stated belt length of at least 50 inches if you plan to
            run, a stated weight limit with a margin, and a warranty you can read before you pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            The colour in the search phrase is only the finish. Prices for different colours of the same
            Goplus machine can differ, so check all of them before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speakers-vs-headphones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Speakers or Headphones: Which Should You Rely On?</h2>
<p>
            For most people who run, the answer is both: the speaker for walking days and classes, and
            headphones for harder sessions. Headphones put the sound next to your ear rather than next
            to the motor, isolate some of the belt noise, and keep the room quiet. The drawbacks are
            sweat, fit while running, and the risk of a cable catching. Wireless sport earbuds or
            open-ear headphones avoid most of those; our
            <a href="/best-headphones-for-treadmill-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">headphones for treadmill running</a> guide has
            the picks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where a speaker genuinely wins is in a shared space with no one to disturb, for walking at
            a desk while listening to a call or a lecture, and for people who find earbuds uncomfortable
            for long sessions. It is also one less thing to charge. If one of those is you, a built-in
            speaker is a reasonable reason to choose between two otherwise similar machines. It is not a
            good reason to choose a weaker treadmill over a stronger one.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more option is worth knowing: a portable Bluetooth speaker on a shelf or the floor near
            the front of the treadmill. It will usually sound better than a console speaker because it
            is larger and further from the belt, and it works with any machine.
          </p>
        </div>

