        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which treadmills have Bluetooth speakers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the folding treadmills we checked on Amazon US, the Horizon T101 Connect, Horizon T202, Sole F63, Goplus 2.25HP 2-in-1, Goplus 3HP and Goplus 3.75HP all state Bluetooth speakers in their listings. The cheapest was a Goplus 2-in-1 at about $114, the most expensive the Sole F63 at about $1,200.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does Bluetooth do on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can mean three things: a speaker that plays audio from your phone, a data connection to a fitness app, and a receiver for a heart-rate strap. A listing that just says "Bluetooth" may have only one of them, so check which before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are treadmill speakers loud enough for running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are fine for walking and easy jogging. At running pace they compete with motor, belt and footfall noise close to the console, so most runners still use headphones for hard sessions. Quieter machines, such as those with brushless motors, give the speaker less to compete with.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the 3HP folding electric treadmill with Bluetooth speaker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is mostly the Goplus 3HP Electric Folding Treadmill, sold under several ASINs in different colours at about $300. Its listing gives 0.6 to 8 mph, a touch screen, a Bluetooth speaker and a self-standing upright fold, but does not state a belt size or weight limit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a Bluetooth FTMS treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                FTMS, or Fitness Machine Service, is the Bluetooth standard that lets a treadmill share speed and incline data with third-party apps such as Zwift and Kinomap. It is separate from Bluetooth audio. A listing that names third-party apps, as the Horizon T101 Connect's does, is the best sign of broad app support.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I add a speaker to a treadmill that does not have one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A portable Bluetooth speaker on a nearby shelf usually sounds better than a built-in console speaker, because it is larger and further from the belt. That makes a built-in speaker a convenience, not a reason to choose a weaker treadmill.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Choosing a Treadmill for Its Speakers</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these comes from treating the speaker as the main feature rather than one of the extras.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying "Bluetooth" and expecting app support</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A speaker and an app connection are different features. If you want Zwift, Peloton or Kinomap, look for those names in the listing, not just the word Bluetooth. Of the five machines here, only the Horizon T101 Connect names third-party apps.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a smaller treadmill for a better speaker</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Speakers are the cheapest part of a treadmill to replace: a portable unit costs a few tens of dollars. Belt length, motor and weight limit cannot be upgraded later. Choose the machine first and treat the speaker as a tiebreaker.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking missing figures in budget listings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Two of the three Goplus listings here leave out a figure that matters — belt size on the 3HP, top speed on the 3.75HP. Ask the seller in writing before you buy, and if the answer does not suit how you run, choose another machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Playing music loud instead of fixing the noise</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If you cannot hear the speaker, the treadmill may be noisier than it should be. Check the belt tension and lubrication, put a dense mat under the frame, and keep the phone volume up. Turning the console to maximum over a dry belt helps no one, least of all neighbours below.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
